WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.499
std::string owns its character data and tracks its length.

2
00:00:03.499 --> 00:00:07.698
That makes it safer and clearer than many raw character-buffer patterns.

3
00:00:07.698 --> 00:00:13.646
But string safety still depends on lifetime, mutation, indexing, encoding assumptions, and the APIs used around it.

4
00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:17.145
std::string_view is a non-owning view into character data.

5
00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:21.344
It is useful because it avoids unnecessary copies and carries a length.

6
00:00:21.344 --> 00:00:26.942
It does not extend the lifetime of the string, buffer, or temporary object it refers to.

7
00:00:26.942 --> 00:00:30.441
If the source disappears, the view becomes unsafe to use.

8
00:00:30.441 --> 00:00:33.940
std::span provides a non-owning view over contiguous elements.

9
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.789
Like string_view, it preserves a length and helps express a range.

10
00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:41.288
Also like string_view, it does not own the underlying storage.

11
00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:47.586
A span into a vector is only valid while the vector's relevant storage remains valid and unmoved.

12
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:49.335
Length-preserving APIs are valuable.

13
00:00:49.335 --> 00:00:55.983
A string_view or span can represent data that contains embedded zero bytes or values that are not null-terminated.

14
00:00:55.983 --> 00:01:00.882
Code should not accidentally pass such data to APIs that expect a different representation.

15
00:01:00.882 --> 00:01:04.381
Views should usually be short-lived parameters or local variables.

16
00:01:04.381 --> 00:01:11.029
Storing them in objects, returning them from factories, or caching them across mutations requires a clear source lifetime guarantee.

17
00:01:11.029 --> 00:01:13.828
Views into temporary strings are especially review-sensitive.

18
00:01:13.828 --> 00:01:15.578
Source mutation can invalidate views.

19
00:01:15.578 --> 00:01:22.925
Appending to a string, resizing a vector, reserving capacity, clearing storage, or moving an owning object can change where data lives.

20
00:01:22.925 --> 00:01:26.774
Existing views, pointers, and references may no longer describe valid data.

21
00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:35.522
The review question is simple: who owns the data, how long does it live, and can it move or change before the view is used?

22
00:01:35.522 --> 00:01:41.120
If those answers are not obvious, prefer an owning type or shorten the view's lifetime.
